      "content": "The primary legal weakness in the \"anti-privilege\" argument is that it often mischaracterizes the privilege as absolute, which is not the current legal consensus. Proponents of the strong privilege side argue that the Supreme Court has always balanced the need for the political function of the President against the claims of the other branches, not that the privilege is a complete shield from all scrutiny. Therefore, arguing it is a \"dangerous erosion\" can come across as an ideological attack on the political reality of the presidency, rather than a strong, grounded legal challenge to the doctrine itself.",
